  "description": "ASERT (ticks variant) vectors generated by the reference implementation. powLimit and all targets are canonical 64-char hex; expectednBits is compact-format hex. The calc_args[*] vectors are BCHN's pow_tests golden vectors (600-tick); each except calc_args[10] and calc_args[11] has a calc_args[*]_60s sibling with nextIdealBlockTime = 60 whose target is exactly 10x the 600s target, clamped to powLimit. calc_args[10]/[11] have no 60s sibling: their 600s pre-clamp target underflowed to zero and was floored to 1, so the 10x derivation does not apply (their 60s result is also 1). The equiv_1M_600s / equiv_1M_60s pair share every input except nextIdealBlockTime; a consumer MUST additionally assert target(equiv_1M_60s) == target(equiv_1M_600s) * 10 exactly. The wrapper array exercises GetNextASERTWorkRequired end-to-end: the testnet min-difficulty branch (strict > at nNextBlockTime > nPrevBlockTime + 2*nextIdealBlockTime, with the threshold derived from the schedule — 1200 s at 600-tick, 120 s at 60-tick), the fPowAllowMinDifficultyBlocks gate, tickDelta derivation from nPrevTick (including a non-zero anchor tick), and the anchor-interval ratio through the full entry point. Wrapper expectednBits are hand-derived golden pins; wrapper_testnet_halflife_fast is reference output with no independent golden. A consumer MUST NOT pass a prev tick before the anchor tick; the reference asserts nPrevTick >= anchor.nTick. The ratio_guard_wrap_low vector pins the interval-ratio overflow guard with a case that neither the shift guard nor the final clamp absorbs. Its shift step leaves nextTarget == 2^253, which passes the shift guard, and k = 600/75 = 8 is a power of two, so the unguarded product is exactly 2^256 and wraps to 0; the floor then yields target 1 (0x01010000), below powLimit, which the final clamp does not correct. The guarded reference returns powLimit (0x1d00ffff) instead, so a consumer MUST NOT accept 0x01010000 for this row. This is the discriminating case: an implementation that drops the pre-ratio guard fails this row, whereas a wrap landing above powLimit would be silently repaired by the final clamp. The guard relies on the schedule constraints (anchor interval 600, tau divides 600), which keep k a whole number in [1, 600]; schedules that relax the divisor rule are out of contract.",
